<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narathihapate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1238">1238</a>–<a href="/years/1287">1287</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Tayokpyemin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narathihapate was king of Pagan from 1256 to 1287. His refusal of the tribute demanded by the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> brought defeat at Ngasaunggyan in 1277 and the loss of the north in the 1280s. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, earning the name Tayokpyemin, 'the king who fled from the Chinese', and was murdered by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a> at Prome in 1287, after which the kingdom broke apart.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Narathihapate was born on 23 April 1238 to Crown Prince Uzana and a commoner concubine from Myittha. Known at the palace as Min Khwe-Chi, he was not in line for the throne even after his father became king in 1251; that place belonged to his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a>, eldest son of the chief queen. When Uzana died in a hunting accident in May 1256 and Thihathu claimed the throne, the court under the chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazathingyan">Yazathingyan</a>, preferring a prince they believed they could control, raised Khwe-Chi instead on 6 May 1256 and had Thihathu executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The young king proved quick-tempered and ruthless. He exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazathingyan">Yazathingyan</a>, then had to recall him to put down revolts in Martaban and Arakan; the minister died on the way home. The treasury, drained for years by the growth of tax-free religious lands, was never restored, and the king built the Mingalazedi Pagoda with forced labour.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, who had conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dali-kingdom">Dali Kingdom</a> in 1253–57, demanded tribute in 1271–72, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> sent a mission of his own in 1273; both were refused. After Mongol forces occupied a Burmese vassal state on the border, the Burmese army sent to recover it was beaten at Ngasaunggyan in April 1277. A fresh invasion took the forts of Ngasaunggyan and Kaungsin in December 1283 and Tagaung in February 1284, and by February 1285 the Mongols had reached Hanlin. Although they had no orders to attack Pagan, the king fled south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>.</p><p>There he settled at Hlegya, west of Prome, unable to win the support of his three sons, who held the ports of Bassein, Dala and Prome, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> and Martaban revolted. In June 1286 he accepted Mongol terms, and in January 1287 his embassy under the chief primate Shin Ditha Pamauk acknowledged Mongol suzerainty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in return for a withdrawal of troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>About a month after the embassy returned in May 1287, the king set out for Pagan but was captured by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a>, Viceroy of Prome, and forced to take poison on 1 July 1287. The kingdom broke apart: the Mon Hanthawaddy kingdom rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, Arakan became independent, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> occupied the north to Tagaung until 1303. The chronicles portray him as a cowardly glutton, but scholars hold that he was an ineffective ruler blamed unfairly for a decline that had begun before his reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Narathihapate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1287 a further invasion coincided with the murder of King <b>Narathihapate</b>, after which the kingdom collapsed into fragments.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bagan, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Pyay, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=1269" aria-label="Bagan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7100 1381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Narathihapate"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7550 1651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7550 1651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=1269">What was here in 1269? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
